<HelpLayout title="Just lifting" description="The fastest path to logging a workout in Badger, without the extra stuff.">
  <h1>Just want to lift?</h1>
  <p>Badger can be as simple as a notebook: go to the gym, write down what you did, leave. This guide sticks to that, so you can start logging today and pick up the rest whenever you like.</p>

  <h2>Your first 5 minutes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the <strong>Today</strong> tab and tap <strong>New workout</strong>.</li>
    <li>Pick an exercise. Scroll the categories or use the search box at the top.</li>
    <li>Enter your weight and reps.</li>
    <li>Tap the <strong>checkmark</strong> to log the set.</li>
    <li>The rest timer starts on its own. Take your break.</li>
    <li>Log your next set, and the one after that.</li>
    <li>When you are done, tap <strong>End workout</strong> to see a quick summary.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Set up nothing</h2>
  <p>You do not need a routine, a program, or any settings to start. Badger includes hundreds of exercises, so most of the time you pick yours and log. If yours is missing, add it as a custom exercise. The only setting that matters is the weight unit, which you picked the first time you opened the app.</p>

  <h2>The 3 things to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>checkmark</strong> logs a set. That is the one button you press over and over.</li>
    <li>The <strong>rest timer</strong> starts by itself after each set. Watch it or ignore it; it does not block anything.</li>
    <li>Your past workouts live on the <strong>History</strong> tab whenever you want to look back.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ignore the rest for now</h2>
  <p>Routines, RPE and RIR, the plate calculator, volume landmarks, progress graphs: all of it stays out of your way until you go looking for it. Leave it alone and the app works like a plain logbook.</p>

  <h2>When you are ready</h2>
  <p>If you start following a set program, <a href="/guides/program-runner/">the program guide</a> shows how to set it up once and have your weights waiting each session. If you get curious about your numbers, <a href="/guides/optimizer/">the data guide</a> shows where your PRs, trends and charts live.</p>

  <p>Want the full picture? See <a href="/guides/overview/">everything Badger does</a>.</p>
</HelpLayout>
